    "lawCode": "Amd §§11-0703 & 11-0702, add §11-0704, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8116\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the transfer\nof a lifetime license for hunting, fishing and trapping\n\nPURPOSE:\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow\nthe transfer of a\nlifetime license upon the death of the purchaser within the first\nyear of purchase, or when an active member of the United States Armed\nForces dies in combat.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection one amends the prohibition on the transfer of sporting\nlicenses to allow transfer under the conditions set in section two.\n\nSection two allows a lifetime sporting license to be transferred if\nall of the following conditions are met:\n\n-The purchaser dies within a year of purchase\n\n-The recipient is a legal resident of the State\n\n-The recipient would otherwise be eligible to purchase the license or\npermit\n\n-The recipient is related to the purchaser by first degree of\nconsanguinity, legal marriage, or is the adopted child or step child\nor step sibling of the purchaser\n\n-The personal representative of the decedent's estate designates the\nrecipient\n\n-The applies for the transfer is made within three years of the issue\ndate of the license -The recipient pays the fee\n\nThis section allows lifetime licenses belonging to veterans who die in\nwar to be transferred outside of the set time limits.\n\nSection three sets the fee for the transfer at $50.00. This section\nalso makes technical changes in subsection lettering.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nLifetime sporting licenses are a privilege many recreational\nenthusiasts enjoy for their simplicity, permanence, and value.\nPurchasing a lifetime license signifies a lifelong commitment to\nconservation and the outdoors.\n\nThis legislation would allow a lifetime license to be transferred in\ncases where the purchaser dies within the first year. However, this\nbill recognizes that death by a veteran serving in war is an unforeseen\ncircumstance, and a courageous sacrifice. The bill allows the license\nto be transferred outside of the time limits set in these\ncircumstances.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred\ntwentieth day after it shall have become law; provided, however, that\neffective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or repeal of any\nrule or regulation necessary for the implementation of this act on\nits effective date are authorized and directed to be made and\ncompleted on or before such effective date.\n\n",
